Lab 8: Integrating Camel with Apache ActiveMQ
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Set up and run an Apache ActiveMQ message broker
Create Apache Camel routes that integrate with ActiveMQ for message-based communication
Implement message producers and consumers using Camel and plain JMS
Test end-to-end message communication through the broker
Apply basic Enterprise Integration Patterns (content-based routing, dead letter handling) using Camel
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Java programming concepts
Familiarity with the Maven build tool
Basic understanding of message-oriented middleware concepts (queues, producers, consumers)
Basic Linux command line skills
Required software (install if not already present):

# Verify Java (JDK 11 or higher required)
java -version

# Verify Maven (3.8+ recommended)
mvn -version

# If Java is missing, install OpenJDK 11
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y openjdk-11-jdk

# If Maven is missing
sudo apt-get install -y maven
This lab downloads and runs Apache ActiveMQ "Classic" 5.x directly as a standalone broker (no package manager dependency), so it works the same way regardless of your Linux distribution.

Lab Environment Setup
Work in an isolated practice environment (a personal VM, container, or lab-provided cloud machine). All actions in this lab are defensive and educational: you are standing up your own broker and routing your own test traffic through it to learn how Camel-based integrations behave, so you can operate and troubleshoot such systems safely in production.

Task 1: Set Up an ActiveMQ Broker
Subtask 1.1: Download and Start ActiveMQ
# Navigate to home directory
cd ~

# Download ActiveMQ Classic (5.x series)
wget https://archive.apache.org/dist/activemq/5.18.3/apache-activemq-5.18.3-bin.tar.gz

# Extract the archive
tar -xzf apache-activemq-5.18.3-bin.tar.gz

# Move to a working location under your home directory
mv apache-activemq-5.18.3 ~/activemq

# Start the broker in the foreground-friendly background mode
cd ~/activemq
./bin/activemq start

# Wait for startup to complete
sleep 10

# Confirm the broker process is running
./bin/activemq status
You should see output similar to ActiveMQ Broker is running (pid ...).

Subtask 1.2: Verify Broker Connectivity
# Confirm the OpenWire port (used by Java/JMS clients) is listening
ss -tlnp 2>/dev/null | grep 61616 || netstat -tlnp 2>/dev/null | grep 61616

# Confirm the web console port is listening
ss -tlnp 2>/dev/null | grep 8161 || netstat -tlnp 2>/dev/null | grep 8161

# Check the web console responds (default credentials: admin/admin)
curl -s -o /dev/null -w "HTTP status: %{http_code}\n" http://localhost:8161/admin/
Deliverable for Task 1: ActiveMQ is running with port 61616 (OpenWire, used by Camel/JMS clients) and port 8161 (web console) both listening. The curl command against /admin/ returns HTTP status: 200 (it may return 401 if you have not authenticated, which still confirms the console is up). The web console is reachable in a browser at http://localhost:8161/admin/ with credentials admin/admin.

