Deliverable for Task 3: In Terminal 2, after sending hello camel from Terminal 1's sender to input.queue, the receiver watching output.queue prints a line similar to:

Received: Processed at 2024-01-15 10:30:45: HELLO CAMEL
Repeat with option 2 in the sender (order.queue) and check either fulfillment.queue or review.queue in the receiver, confirming the content-based routing choice worked. Repeat several times with option 3 (risky.queue) checked against success.queue and dead.letter.queue to observe both the success path and the simulated failure path after redeliveries are exhausted.

Expected Outcomes
A running ActiveMQ broker accepting connections on port 61616 with a functioning web console on port 8161
A compiled Camel application whose routes consume from ActiveMQ queues, transform messages, and route them based on content
Verified end-to-end message flow: a message sent by MessageSender is transformed by a Camel route and successfully read back by MessageReceiver from the correct destination queue
Troubleshooting
Symptom: MessageSender or MessageReceiver throws java.net.ConnectException or hangs on connect. Diagnostic hint: Confirm the broker is actually running with ~/activemq/bin/activemq status and that port 61616 is listening; the Camel application and the JMS utilities all assume the broker is already up.
Symptom: The receiver reports "No message received... within timeout" even though you sent a message. Diagnostic hint: Check the Terminal 1 application log (or logs/camel-activemq.log) for route errors or exceptions, and confirm you sent to the correct source queue that feeds the destination queue you are watching (for example, input.queue feeds output.queue, not the reverse).
Conclusion
In this lab you deployed a standalone Apache ActiveMQ broker, built a Maven-based Apache Camel application with JMS routes that consume, transform, and content-route messages, and validated the integration by driving test traffic through the broker with dedicated sender and receiver utilities. Along the way you exercised core Enterprise Integration Patterns including message transformation, content-based routing, and dead-letter error handling with redelivery. You now have a working, self-contained reference for how Camel routes interact with an ActiveMQ broker, which is the foundation for building and troubleshooting more complex messaging integrations.
